 "explanation": "Tempester Voice, items 1, 2 and 6: parser hardening against the permanent mute, the queue full test on the patch burst and command paths, the dump stream reset at the Main's start. Resynchronisation: a burst word is two sound parameters, each at most 254, so the word 0xFF00 is never burst data; it is the second word of every read the Main makes ([routine]: Timer 3's 0xF300 poll, the dumps). Received in patch mode, it means the burst was cut: patch mode ends as after a whole burst, the word is a command (none). Self-calibration: a 0xFD word is never queued from the SPI any more. The Main's calibration ([routine]) sends 0xFD00 and then, interrupts off, reads 0xF900 from the same chip; only that pair (0xFD00 as the previous command word, then 0xF900) queues 0xFD00, so the voice starts its calibration at the first 0xF900 read, one poll later than DSI's. Burst data cannot form the pair: it needs two consecutive odd parameters both above 248. Item 6: the Main sends 0xF5nn (voice number) only at its start-up, when its full-backup stream positions (n°62) are 0: the voice's dump stream (pointer [address], byte phase [address]) goes back to PC 0 there too, so a backup stays aligned after the Main restarts alone. Speed: PATCHFAST replaces DSI's patch path (same queue words, same [address] counter and end) and COMMAND replaces DSI's linear decoding ([address], up to 15 compares before a handler): a word below 0xEA00 is queued at once (DSI only ignores 0xB700 there), a word from 0xEA00 jumps through a table on its high byte to DSI's own handler or to the queue. Both are faster than DSI's code: the SPI interrupt keeps up with the Main's back-to-back words (queue_full.s), and every reply (0xEC00, 0xED00, 0xF300, 0xF400, 0xF800, 0xF900, 0xFB00, 0xFE00) is in SPI1BUF earlier than with DSI, before the Main starts its 0xFF00 read (104.5 voice cycles after the end of the command word, [routine] on the simulated Main). The guards (0xFD, 0xF5, the 0xFD00 + 0xF900 pair) cost nothing before a reply: the pair is checked after the 0xF900 reply is written.",
